MCP (Model Context Protocol) is Anthropic's open-source protocol that lets AI assistants like Claude connect to external data sources and tools. Think of it as a standardized way for AI models to interact with your local files, databases, APIs, and other resources.
What MCP does:
Provides AI models with controlled access to external context (files, databases, APIs)
Lets you build "servers" that expose specific resources or tools
Works across different AI applications that support the protocol
Setting up MCP in Cursor:
Cursor has built-in MCP support. Here's how to set it up:
Find or create an MCP server - You can use existing ones (there are servers for filesystem access, GitHub, databases, etc.) or build your own. Anthropic maintains a repository of community servers.
Configure Cursor - Add MCP server configuration to Cursor's settings. This typically goes in your Cursor configuration file (similar to VS Code's settings.json):
json
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "filesystem": {
      "command": "npx",
      "args": ["-y", "@modelcontextprotocol/server-filesystem", "/path/to/allowed/directory"]
    }
  }
}
Install dependencies - Some MCP servers require Node.js, Python, or other runtimes depending on what they're built with.
Restart Cursor - The MCP servers should start automatically when Cursor launches.
Common use cases for you:
Accessing project documentation or notes outside your codebase
Connecting to databases for schema information
Integrating with Nike's internal tools or APIs (if you build custom servers)
Reading research materials or writing references for your fiction projects
